module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.witness_that_fails_to_compile_is_absent_rather_than_red

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data witness_that_fails_to_compile_is_absent_rather_than_red: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "witness_that_fails_to_compile_is_absent_rather_than_red" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a witness that fails to COMPILE is ABSENT rather than red, and running its SUBJECT proves nothing about it** (a claim file is committed, reviewed and merged while it cannot compile, so none of its assertions ever execute and the guarantee it was written to carry does not exist; the class it polices reports as walled while nothing walls it, which is worse than an absent witness because the file is cited as coverage by its own author and by its reviewers).",
    "WHY IT IS A CLASS AND NOT A SLIP: every ordinary signal is compatible with the dead file and NONE OF THEM IS MALFUNCTIONING. It emits no advisories, because a file that does not compile contributes none. It fails no assertions, because none run. It is ABSENT from the floor disposition rather than failing in it, so a disposition read shows nothing to investigate. `cargo fmt` is green because the file is well-formed text. The push is green. And a reviewer can APPROVE on a wholly correct reading of the source, because READING DOES NOT COMPILE.",
    "THE CORE IS A TWO-CLAIM COLLAPSE: `the compiler behaves correctly` and `the witness that enrols that behaviour as executing evidence works` are DIFFERENT CLAIMS, and running the subject as a direct fixture establishes only the first. The mechanism of the misread is that holding the stronger claim's output makes the weaker one feel already answered — the author has the passing fixtures in hand, which is exactly why the file meant to carry them never gets checked.",
    "RECOGNITION RULE: ask what would have caught it, and IF THE ONLY ANSWER IS A RUN AIMED AT A DIFFERENT QUESTION, the class has no dedicated detector. The specimen surfaced from a whole-tree advisory census re-derivation chasing an unrelated hypothesis about a denominator, as blocking error number one, after the arms were green, the PR was open and an APPROVE was already recorded. THE ONE INSTRUMENT THAT SEES IT is a whole-tree compile INCLUDING the test roots; compiling the subject, or entry-scoping to the witness itself, does not.",
    "SECOND SPECIMEN, AND IT MOVES THE ORIGIN OF THE NON-COMPILATION OUT OF THE WITNESS FILE. gunbc#10966 (ea18354a) added five REQUIRED fields -- head_branch, event, run_attempt, actor, triggering_actor -- to `extdeps.github.workflow_runs` `WorkflowRun`, and did not update the four `WorkflowRun` literals in `dag/test/claim/superseded_run_starvation_census_witness_test.dag`, which imports that type. From ea18354a onward the witness file could not compile: `missing required field run_attempt`, `missing required field event`, and four more. Every signal the first specimen describes held again -- fmt green, floor green, no disposition row to read -- and one more was added: the witness own diff was EMPTY, so no reviewer of the witness was ever presented with it, and the reviewer of gunbc#10966 saw a type edit with no witness named in it. The floor stayed green because `test.claim.superseded_run_starvation_census_witness` matches no entry in `v2.workflow.required_floor` `required_gate_prefixes`, and a push to main contributes no changed-witness module seeds, so the module sat OUTSIDE the gate closure assembled in `required_floor_runner` `closure_seeds`. The producer that re-derives this standing for any head is the required-CI claim executor on the witnesses lane (`gunbc.witness_floor_workflow`), whose `gate-closure` and `site-projection` phase lines carry the closure and decline populations and whose `required_floor_disposition` artifact carries the per-identity disposition. Repaired in the PR that files this receipt.",
    "WHAT THE SECOND SPECIMEN ADDS TO THE TRIGGER, and it is a strengthening rather than a new class: the trigger already names the right capability -- COMPILE every claim-root file, do not merely execute the ones that compile -- and this specimen shows the capability must be a WITHIN-TREE JOIN rather than a per-change one. No reading of the gunbc#10966 diff alone is sufficient, because the construction sites that stopped compiling are in files the diff does not touch; the join is between a type whose required fields changed and every construction site of that type in the tree, which is the same `Node`-tree reachability the namespace authority already computes. A per-file or per-diff compile roster would have missed this exactly as the execution roster did.",
    "MEASURED ON THE REPAIR ITSELF, and it is the sharpest evidence in this row: THE PER-PR SELECTOR CANNOT REACH A DEFECT THAT LIVES IN A DATA FIXTURE. The PR repairing the second specimen edited `WorkflowRun` literals in `test.claim.superseded_run_starvation_census_witness` and `test.claim.github_workflow_runs_witness`. The floor planted exactly one of their claims: the `required_floor_disposition` artifact -- produced by the required-CI claim executor on the witnesses lane, columns `identity`, `disposition`, `matched_prefix`, `outcome` -- carries `test.claim.superseded_run_starvation_census_witness.absent_job_rosters_remain_a_coverage_obligation` at `disposition=planned_as_changed_witness` and `outcome=passed`, and EVERY OTHER claim in that module at `declined_outside_required_gate`, and every claim in `test.claim.github_workflow_runs_witness` at `declined_outside_gate_closure` -- all of them `outcome=not_executed`. The populations are not transcribed here because the artifact is the producer that re-derives them per head.",

    "THE ONE CLAIM THAT RAN IS THE ONE CLAIM THAT COULD NOT HAVE CAUGHT THE DEFECT. absent_job_rosters_remain_a_coverage_obligation calls dead_head_start_standing with two absent timestamps; it constructs no WorkflowRun and reads none of the repaired literals. So the floor green on that head is true and says nothing whatever about the repair -- the same shape as a_positive_control_certifies_the_defect_it_exists_to_catch, arrived at from the selection side rather than the authoring side.",

    "WHY IT SELECTED THAT ONE, and the mechanism is decidable rather than guessed. The selector maps each changed line to the enclosing declaration SPAN (decl start to next decl start minus one) and routes by declaration kind: a changed line inside a test fn joins edited_test_fns, which becomes the planned changed-witness set; a changed line inside a data declaration joins overlapping_data_items, which feeds only rerun_frontier_nodes_for_entry -- a memo/rerun frontier for incremental reuse -- and PLANS NO WITNESS. EVERY hunk in that repair that edited a literal fell inside a `data` declaration and therefore planned nothing. The SOLE hunk that planned anything was an annotation block authored between a test fn and the following data declaration, so it fell inside the PRECEDING test fn span and planned that fn by pure adjacency -- the claim named in the row above, which reads none of the repaired literals. Had the annotation been written below the data declaration instead of above it, the floor would have planned ZERO witnesses and been green over an untouched subject.",

    "THE CONSEQUENCE FOR THE TRIGGER: a per-PR changed-witness selector is not a weaker version of the capability this row needs, it is a DIFFERENT capability that this class routes around by construction. Every instance of this class is a construction site -- a literal, a fixture, a data row -- and construction sites live in data declarations, which the selector deliberately does not plan. So the trigger is unchanged and is reinforced: the floor must COMPILE every claim-root file as a population, and no amount of improvement to diff-keyed selection substitutes for it.",
    "Rung found at 1, mitigatable, held only by an unrelated run happening to range over the file, which is not a mechanism. CEILING 3, structurally guaranteed: the population is decidable and closed — every file under the claim roots — so a required step that compiles them and refuses a non-compiling claim file makes the state unwritable in an `Accepted` tree. NEXT TRIGGER, capability and not artifact: the required floor must COMPILE every claim-root file and refuse a claim file that does not compile, rather than only executing the claim files that compile — AN EXECUTION ROSTER KEYED TO COMPILING FILES CANNOT SEE A FILE THAT FELL OUT OF IT, the same shape as a check whose population IS its own roster. Specimen gunbc#10337: `brand_nominal_identity_witness_test` named a host builtin in its import list; four direct fixtures had already proven the compiler refuses, and the witness enrolling them had never run.",
    "THE TRIGGER'S GRAIN IS NARROWER THAN THE RULED CAPABILITY, AND THIS RECEIPT WIDENS IT RATHER THAN LETTING THE NARROW TEXT STAND (sunny-tern-813 ruling, relayed and verified by crisp-lynx-452, 2026-09-11; filed from the joint-incompatibility scope, gunbc.plans.joint_incompatibility_claim_join section 2.3). The row above names the capability as `COMPILE every claim-root file`. The ruled capability is WIDER: compile every CONSTRUCTION SITE of a type whose required fields changed, AND every claim-root file regardless of gate closure -- and a construction site can live in a module that is not a claim root at all. DESIGN section 4b(3): a trigger naming less than the capability it restores will be satisfied while the capability stays dead, and the review tell is a grain mismatch between the loss sentence and the trigger sentence. The loss sentence of the second specimen is construction-site grain (`every construction site of that type in the tree`); the trigger sentence is claim-root-file grain. THAT IS THE MISMATCH. So the trigger, as of this receipt, reads at the ruled width: THE REQUIRED FLOOR MUST COMPILE EVERY CLAIM-ROOT FILE AND EVERY CONSTRUCTION SITE OF A TYPE WHOSE DECLARED FIELD SET CHANGED BETWEEN BASE AND HEAD, REGARDLESS OF GATE CLOSURE, AND REFUSE ANY THAT DOES NOT COMPILE. Discharging the narrower sentence alone does not retire this row.",
    "WHAT THE JOINT-INCOMPATIBILITY SCOPE ESTABLISHED ABOUT THIS ROW, so the two are not conflated: the cross-change join over the declaration index (v1_compiler.bin.joint_claim_join) keys on a module's EXPORT SURFACE -- declared, variants, reexported -- and a field is not a surface entry, so that join is silent on every specimen of this row by construction; the two classes share a shape (a change compiled against a population its diff does not name) and not a mechanism. The within-tree half needs no open-PR set and no cross-change anything: the removed side is `decl_fields` base-minus-head per declaration, derivable from the same base reconstruction the wave wall performs, and the claiming side needs no precise construction-site channel because the COMPILE is the oracle and the index only selects what to compile -- every module whose `referenced` or `authored_type_references` names the changed declaration, where over-collection costs compile time and never a false finding. That is the selection this row's widened trigger names, and it is this row's to discharge, not a checkpoint of the join's plan. THE BOUNDARY OF THAT TRIGGER, STATED SO THE WORD POPULATION IS NOT LATER READ AS MORE THAN IT SAYS: the population compile establishes COMPILABILITY of the claim-root population and NOT EVALUATION of it. A claim file that compiles and whose `test fn`s are declared but absent from the entry's conjunction sails through that wall untouched (cool-badger-34, 2026-09-11: four such fns in a witness module type-checked, read as coverage, were never evaluated, and turned the suite red when enrolled). That second hole -- compiles YES, evaluated NO -- is owned by the neighbour row gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.discriminating_arm_built_but_never_enrolled, whose detector counts constructed fixtures against enrolled assertions; it is not this row's and this trigger does not close it.",
    "THIRD SPECIMEN, TYPE GROWTH IN ANOTHER MODULE. gunbc#11194 at d75c000465d7ec26bc4b86fd28f212c7a776193d grew `gunbc.product.fabric.provider_admission` `ProviderUseRefusal` in ANOTHER MODULE. `accepted_of` was exhaustive for the constructors then declared when it landed. Required CI stayed green. `gunbc compile --entry` on `dag/test/claim/fabric/common_paper_csa_agreement_witness_test.dag` at that commit refused `NonExhaustiveMatch` (missing PostureJurisdictionsUnread, TransferOrLocationUnresolved, ProviderAccessPathUnsupported). TWO FACES, AND THEY MUST NOT BE COLLAPSED. At d75c000 that match file WAS in the changed set -- two deletions, a data row AND a field inside `fn posture`, which is non-data non-test-fn grain -- so `FloorDiffEdits.touched_entry_files` seeding of Strict prepare (gunbc#11252, closed, recoverable at 23d57eb) would have pulled its closure and fired exhaustiveness on THIS specimen. That work walks DEPENDENCY edges only (import + dotted-reference + bare-reference); it is option A's MIRROR, not B, and not a dependents walk. What it would NOT catch is the harder half of the class: arms grow in another module and the match file has NO DIFF AT ALL. Seeding the arm-adding module pulls its dependencies; the stale match is its dependent. That half needs A, the same seeding step with the edge index read the other way -- not a second mechanism, not built, PURPOSE admission still owed to the operator.",
    "ONE-SITE BOUND, AND ONLY THAT SCOPE. Among modules that NAME `ProviderUseRefusal` at d75c000, compiled `--entry` rather than grepped: the stale match is `accepted_of` (`NonExhaustiveMatch`). The other named consumers (`provider_admission_witness_test` `refusal_tag`/`refusal_located`, in-module `refusal_reason`, `grant_issue.dag`, `selection.dag`) compiled clean -- they had been updated with the type. THAT MEMBERSHIP BOUNDS THAT COPRODUCT'S NAMED CONSUMERS AT THAT COMMIT. It says the lane that grew the type mostly did its job and this site was missed. It says nothing about other coproducts that have grown, which is the actual class. Quoting the missed site as a class size is a misread of the census.",
    "THE ZERO THAT WAS REFUSED, NAMED AS THE TEACHING EXAMPLE. A whole-corpus `gunbc compile` without `--entry` refused at `WholeCorpusCompileBudgetBelowMeasuredDemand`. Infer never started. The log contained no `NonExhaustiveMatch` lines. That empty log was AVAILABLE, TRUE as the contents of a log, and reporting it would have closed `the corpus currently has zero` in the census's favour with nobody able to tell. It was refused: silence from a run that never typechecked is the killed-process memorial the admission refusal exists to prevent, the same distinction this investigation is about -- absent versus never-looked -- applied to this instrument rather than to someone else's code. The specimen shows a checker that never ran; this empty log shows the false green you get when you read its silence as a result.",
    "THE UNMEASURED WHOLE-CORPUS COMPILE IS A DEPENDENCY WITH AN OWNER, NOT A PERMANENT CEILING. The admission refusal is `WholeCorpusCompileBudgetBelowMeasuredDemand` (`declared-unverified` against measured demand). nimble-badger-98 owns whole-corpus compile admission; gunbc#11227 (`Whole-corpus compile admission: demand is a fact of the root compiled (MeasuredForRoot | UnmeasuredRoot)`) is the path: demand must be a fact of the root compiled rather than a population-blind constant, and the self-host plan names the row instead of a transcribed budget. `whole-tree compile is not the oracle on this runner` is that outstanding item, not a wall.",
    "THE FLOOR'S PREPARED COMPILED SUBJECT IS THE GATE CLOSURE, NOT THE WITNESS CORPUS, SETTLED BY EXECUTION NOT BY THE STEP NAME. `gunbc.witness.witness_floor_workflow` names the step `All witnesses (one prepared subject, one fold)`. The producer that actually prepares is `v1.compiler.cli_run` `assemble_prepared_subject_closure` via `required_floor_runner` `closure_seeds` over `v2.workflow.required_floor` `required_gate_prefixes` plus changed-witness module seeds plus the floor's runtime-authority modules. Identity-level observation on a clean tree (no changed-witness seeds) against that assemble: `test.claim.fabric.common_paper_csa_agreement_witness` present in `full_inventory` and ABSENT from the prepared keep set; positive control `test.claim.match_exhaustiveness_coproduct_witness` present in the prepared keep set. Inventory sizes are not transcribed; re-derive them from that producer. Discovery walks the corpus; Strict typecheck is the keep set. Discriminating plant: extra `ProviderUseRefusal` arm in `provider_admission.dag`, in-module `refusal_reason` updated, victim file untouched. After the plant the victim was still absent from the prepared keep set. Term (b) therefore has a hole: a name/type growth that only reds when the unplanned witness is typechecked is invisible to the floor's compiled subject. DuplicateModule as a discriminator, and the `required_gate_prefixes` widening refusal, live on [[check_reachable_only_from_an_entry_point_the_context_never_calls]] -- one home, cited rather than copied.",
    "DISCOVERY ENUMERATES; IT DOES NOT RESOLVE. The producing run is required-floor 34740787182 on gunbc#11194; its `required_floor_disposition` artifact is the producer that re-derives discovered / executed / not_executed membership per identity. That is not a prepared-subject-equals-planned-set reading -- discovery walks `assemble_prepared_subject_closure`'s `full_inventory` and the floor Strict-resolves only the gate keep set. The per-file authority is `v2.workflow.floor_discovery_source_authority` `discover_floor_rows_for_source(repo_path, content)`: it splits `content` on newlines and enrolls names from lines that start with `test fn ` / `test data ` (`floor_discovery_scan_test_decl_names`). The producer_frame is the discovery AUTHORITY module's scope, already in the gate closure; the discovered source files are never handed to `resolved_graph_from_sources`. You can name an identity without resolving the module that declares it. `v2.workflow.floor_naming_hygiene` still calls that line scan a `SECOND PARSER`. Executed, not inferred: `gunbc run` of `floor_discovery_scan_test_decl_names` on `test fn planted_dark_population() -> Bool { this_name_is_not_declared() }` returned `[planted_dark_population]` -- a name enrolled from bytes whose callee does not resolve.",

    "IDENTITIES DISCOVERED OUTSIDE THE KEEP SET ARE DARK TO RESOLUTION, NOT DARK IN EVERY RESPECT. Disposition `not_executed` on that artifact must not be quoted as unwatched. Merge-path standing of `gunbc.bare_name_fork_lens` is [[stale_claim_survives_its_own_correct_edit]] -- not restated here. What still needs a RESOLVED module -- non-exhaustive matches, variant-arm forks, resolve-time defects -- is dark to Infer for keep-set-absent identities. A green disposition cannot tell `resolved and fine` from `never resolved`. DuplicateModule as a detector is [[check_reachable_only_from_an_entry_point_the_context_never_calls]], not this row.",

    "THE 68-MINUTE FLOOR RUN WAS DECLINED BECAUSE IT COULD NOT HAVE ANSWERED. A green floor's outcome vocabulary is only `not_executed` / `passed` / `route-gap-before-verdict` / `known-red-held`. There is no arm distinguishing resolved-and-fine from never-resolved, so spending the hour would have produced a green that a reader would take as coverage -- the exact confusion this investigation exists to distrust. The victim also sits outside the keep set, so Strict-prepare would never have seen a plant in that file. Three instrument choices, each against the cheaper or more dramatic option: refuse DuplicateModule as the discriminator ([[check_reachable_only_from_an_entry_point_the_context_never_calls]]), decline to let Strict SIGKILL reverse membership, decline the floor run that could not discriminate.",
    "THE SAME OUT-OF-CLOSURE GAP ADMITS A SECOND DEFECT CLASS, AND THIS TIME THE WALL WAS A REVIEW RATHER THAN CI (gunbc#11268, closed 2026-09-13; review 65408). This row already names test.claim.superseded_run_starvation_census_witness as its second specimen for the NON-COMPILING class. This receipt records that the same module, sitting outside v2.workflow.required_floor required_gate_prefixes for the same reason, would also have admitted a DUPLICATE FUNCTION BINDING to main green -- a different invalid state reaching the same silence, which is what makes the gap a class rather than a property of one defect.\n\nWHAT WAS AUTHORED: a second binding of the name superseded_run_starvation_census in gunbc.superseded_run_starvation_census, while func superseded_run_starvation_census already stood in that module. One name bound twice in one module is the DESIGN section 3 violation in its most literal form and a duplicate-binding compile hazard, and it was authored in a lane whose whole purpose was to remove a dangling declaration.\n\nTHE ENABLING BLIND SPOT IS LEXICAL AND IS WORTH MORE THAN THE INCIDENT: .dag declares functions with BOTH fn and func, and the string func foo does not contain the substring fn. So a search anchored on fn -- caret fn space for a module outline, or fn dot-star name for one symbol -- cannot match a func declaration, and returns a confident empty result rather than an error. func is used on the order of 557 times corpus-wide, so this is not an exotic spelling. The unification is tracked at gunbc#10970 and gunbc#10850; until it lands, a symbol search matching only on fn is half a search.\n\nA SECOND ERROR COMPOUNDED IT, AND IT IS ABOUT REPORTING RATHER THAN SEARCHING: the dangling-declaration claim came from a grep that found the symbol and then excluded the defining module, answering the question is this referenced OUTSIDE its module. That question was sound and its answer was correct. It was then reported as no producer anywhere in the corpus, which is a different and false claim. An exclusion applied inside a search has to survive into the conclusion drawn from it.\n\nWHY IT BELONGS ON THIS ROW: every ordinary signal was again compatible with the defect. The witness file compiles, fmt is green, and the floor would have been green because the module is outside the gate closure -- so the duplicate binding would have been carried by review diligence alone. It was, and the review named the line and the exact grep that disproved the claim. That is the class this row exists to keep visible: the module is not merely uncovered for one defect, it is uncovered, and the trigger this row already carries -- a required step that compiles every file under the claim roots and refuses a non-compiling one -- would close this instance too, because a duplicate binding in that module is precisely a compile refusal nobody is asking for.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
